                    DIGITAL AND MITEL JOIN FORCES
                TO INTEGRATE COMPUTERS AND TELEPHONY


             Joint Development of CTI Products to Offer
                    "One Stop Shopping" Solution


KANATA, ONTARIO/MAYNARD, MASS. -- March 7, 1995 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation and Mitel Corporation today signed a joint development 
agreement which is expected to establish the two companies as a key 
force in the burgeoning computer telephony integration (CTI) 
marketplace. This rapidly expanding market is expected to grow from 
less than $2 billion in 1993 to $5.6 billion in 1997, according to 
BIS Strategic Decisions of Norwell, Massachusetts.

     "Mitel and Digital bring a world-class act of complementary 
technology and business skills to the Computer Telephony 
marketplace," commented Peter Bohacek, vice president of the CTI 
Products Group at Mitel Corporation. "This alliance will work to 
advance the convergence of the computer and telephone into new and 
better ways to communicate."

     Under the development agreement, the two companies jointly 
agree to combine their expertise in an effort to develop a family of 
CTI computer products, integrating computing and communications 
devices into a single unit. Both parties will have an opportunity to 
commercialize any products developed under this agreement. 
CTI technology soon will allow users easy access to a variety of 
services, including integrated phone, fax, voice mail and electronic 
mail -- at the desktop or remotely from any touch tone phone.

     Jim Burton from CT Link in Boston, Massachusetts said, "Mitel 
and Digital are both pioneers in the development of CTI technology. 
This is a natural and complementary alliance that will undoubtedly 
further the development of the CTI marketplace."

     "This alliance will play a pivotal role in eliminating 
traditional computer and telephone information access barriers," 
said Philippe Ribeyre, Digital's vice president, Workstation 
Business Segment. "By blurring the distinction between telephone 
keypad and the computer keyboard, users will have complete command 
of their computer and telephone resources -- whether from the desk, 
the car, the air or the hotel."

     For example, a traveling executive will be able to retrieve 
voice mail and electronic mail messages over the phone, or fax the 
messages to his or her hotel, simply by using the telephone keypad," 
Ribeyre continued. "Whether the users are 'road warriors', or 
located in workgroups or large departments, this is one of many 
applications which will be available through this technology."

     Mitel Corporation is an international telecommunications 
solutions supplier, providing customer premise equipment, 
semiconductors, public switching systems, network enhancement  and 
gateway products, PC communications products and systems development 
software components. The company is active in major growth markets 
such as wireless, computer telephone integration, and emerging 
technology systems. Mitel is committed to advancing people-to-people 
communications in an open-distributed environment.

