            DIGITAL'S LINKWORKS VERSION 3 OFFERS POWERFUL
                   NEW CAPABILITIES FOR DEVELOPERS
                    OF CUSTOM BUSINESS SOLUTIONS


MAYNARD, Mass. March 7, 1995 Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced a major new version of its LinkWorks solution framework 
software. For the first time, users can take advantage of software 
component technology that combines information on the desktop with 
information from business applications, data warehouses and document 
databases.

     LinkWorks offers a complete object-oriented environment to 
build solutions for critical business processes such as client 
relations, customer service, project management and patient care. 
This information is maintained in an application's original file 
format by intelligent containers. Version 3 also features an 
improved user interface, expanded workflow capabilities, simplified 
system administration, improved performance, and built-in 
integration for popular mail applications and ALL-IN-1 file cabinet 
access.

     The Version 3 development and deployment environment now 
enables software developers to build applications using software 
components, application programming interfaces, and a scripting 
language. Software Components Easily Created, Distributed, and Installed

     "With Version 3, the LinkWorks object-oriented engine is opened 
up to the developer community," said Dilip Phadke, group marketing 
manager of Digital's workgroup integration products. "This openness 
dramatically strengthens the capabilities of Digital's business 
partners to easily develop and deploy reusable business solutions 
with the LinkWorks software component technology."

     LinkWorks software components are reusable plug-and-play 
modules that can be combined to create custom business solutions 
which are easily distributed via CD-ROM or electronic mail and 
installed simply by dragging and dropping it onto the LinkWorks 
desktop.

     Application programming interfaces enable developers to extend 
existing applications with LinkWorks functionality, or to extend the 
LinkWorks framework itself, using tools such as Microsoft Visual 
BASIC or the C and C++ programming languages. Industry-standard 
mechanisms such as DDE (dynamic data exchange), OLE2 (object linking 
and embedding) and DLL (dynamic linked libraries) control 
interaction between applications and LinkWorks.

     The new scripting language creates custom functionality rapidly 
by automatically recording sequences of user actions into scripts, 
which can be replayed, edited and shared with other LinkWorks users. 
The scripting language creates screen location independent scripts 
that run on any LinkWorks platform.

     Along with the new development technology included in the 
LinkWorks product, Digital also announced a LinkWorks Software 
Developer Kit consisting of tutorial and reference documentation, 
example applications, extensive online help for the software 
developer, and customizable source versions for the online user 
help.
     
Award-winning LinkWorks Meets Requirements of Global Enterprises

     To address the business requirements of global enterprises, 
LinkWorks is available in 24 languages, including major Asian 
languages. LinkWorks won BYTE magazine's 1993 "Best of COMDEX" Award 
for Connectivity Software and the "Software for Europe" Gold Award 
at the CeBIT'94 Fair in Hannover, Germany.

     LinkWorks software is a client/server framework for creating 
and delivering business solutions. Client support includes Microsoft 
Windows, Macintosh, OS/2, and Motif workstations. Servers include 
the SCO UNIX (on Intel), Windows NT Server (on Intel and Alpha), DEC 
OSF/1 UNIX (on Alpha), OpenVMS (on Alpha), HP-UX and IBM AIX 
platforms. LinkWorks utilizes relational database software from 
several vendors including CA/Ingres, Informix, and Oracle.

Pricing and Availability

     LinkWorks Version 3 pricing remains unchanged. A client or 
server license including media and documentation is $299, or $499 
including the database software. License-only prices are $30 less. 
Upgrades from Version 2.1 to Version 3 are $99 (license only), or 
$149 (including media and documentation). The LinkWorks Software 
Developer Kit is $99 and will be available this month. The source 
version of the user documentation is $7500 and will be available 
this month. The LinkWorks Version 3 for Windows client and LinkWorks 
Version 3 for DEC OSF/1 UNIX on Alpha server are available 
immediately. Other clients and servers listed above will be released 
throughout 1995.

     LinkWorks information is available on Digital's Internet World 
Wide Web server at http://www.digital.com/info/linkworks/.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
