        DIGITAL PRIORIS PC SERVERS EXCEL IN PRICE/PERFORMANCE

 
     AIM Technology's Spring UNIX System Price/Performance Guide
           Publishes Outstanding Ratings for Prioris Line


MAYNARD, Mass. -- March 8, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that three of its Prioris PC servers have achieved 
outstanding price/performance ratings, according to a quarterly 
report by AIM Technology, an independent testing organization 
specializing in UNIX performance tools and services.  Digital 
Prioris servers took top honors in shared system and file server 
categories for systems priced less than $50,000.  

     According to results published in the Spring, 1995, edition of 
AIM Technology's UNIX System Price/Performance Guide, Digital's 
Prioris systems outperformed major worldwide UNIX system 
manufacturers in AIM's tests, including Compaq Computer Corp., 
Zenith Data Systems, AST Research, Tricord Systems and Ing. C. 
Olivetti & C.S.p.A.

          Best Price/Sustained Performance Under $50,000 -
    Systems that reach their maximum capacity for the least cost

     System			       Mix		Rank *
     Digital Prioris HX 590DP	       Shared system	1
     Digital Prioris XL Server 590     Shared system	2           
     Digital Prioris XL Server 590     File server	1
     Digital Prioris MTE Server 4100   File server	2

             Best Sustained Performance under $50,000 -
   Systems that maintain high performance under heavy system loads


     System			       Mix		Rank *	    
     Digital Prioris XL Server 590     File server	2

     * 1 is the highest rank awarded by AIM Technology

     The Prioris HX 590DP server was highly ranked for Best 
Sustained Performance in the shared system mix, for servers priced 
less than $50,000.  The Prioris XL Server 566 was also highly rated 
for Best Peak Performance and Best Sustained Performance for file 
servers priced less $15,000. 

     All multiuser systems were tested using the AIM Multiuser 
Benchmark Suite VII, which tests servers running features required 
by modern open computing systems in multiuser environments.  The AIM 
suite simulates a system's application environment and tests 
performance under various applications loads and mixes. For example, 
the "shared system" mix tests multiuser performance in a standard 
office environment encompassing word processing, spreadsheet, email, 
database, payroll and data processing applications. The "file 
server" mix tests system perfomance under heavy, non-network, 
integer and I/O loads. 

     "The test results from AIM Technology strengthen Digital's 
existing reputation for excellent price/performance and subsequent 
customer value," said Duane Dickhut, vice president of Digital's PC 
Server Product Line.  "The exceptional scalability and reliability 
of the Prioris line of servers, along with Digital's commitment to 
global service and support, position the systems among the leaders 
in the Intel-based server marketplace."

     According to Jim Geers, president of AIM Technology, "The UNIX 
System Price Performance Guide was developed to help manufacturers 
and end-users measure and identify the performance of multiuser 
systems in different application environments.  Digital's superior 
performance affirms that the company is a strong force in today's 
UNIX market."

     Digital's Prioris HX systems are optimized to meet customers' 
needs today and in the future with processor scalability and 
subsystem upgradeablity.  The Prioris HX systems are targeted at IS 
managers running business-critical applications, network operating 
systems, and  industry standard databases.  Prioris systems are 
available through Digital's channel partners, including master 
resellers, wholesalers, distributors, dealers, VARs and system 
integrators, as well as through Digital's direct sales organization.

