            DIGITAL TO SUPPLY COMMON DESKTOP ENVIRONMENT
                         ON UNIX(R) SYSTEMS


          ...All Alpha systems to support industry standard
                        user environment... 


DALLAS, TX -- March 14, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced that it will include the Common Desktop Environment (CDE) 
on all members of its UNIX Alpha workstation and server families in 
the second half of 1995. The Common Desktop Environment was unveiled 
at the UniForum '95 Conference and Exhibition here today. Digital 
continues to ship an advanced developers kit as part of its current 
operating system release, Digital UNIX V3.2*.

     "Our customers and software partners have asked the industry's 
system suppliers to simplify development and deployment of 
applications across platforms and to help reduce the training burden 
as users move from one system to another," said Don Harbert, vice 
president of Digital's UNIX Business Unit. "The availability of CDE 
on our UNIX platforms assures these customers that Digital's systems 
meet that goal." 

     CDE offers users the benefits of ease of use with a common 
"look and feel" across a broad range of platforms. With its high 
degree of flexibility, CDE is easily customized to meet specific 
application needs. The design enables ready adaptation to 
client/server computing environments, while continuing to support 
existing UNIX applications.  For application developers, CDE offers 
a consistent set of programming interfaces which helps ensure 
application portability and interoperability among systems in 
heterogeneous operating environments. 

     Digital's implementation of CDE is provided on Digital UNIX, 
the most standards-compliant UNIX in the market today. With the 
addition of CDE, Digital offers users and developers a programming 
environment which will be common across the industry.  

     "CDE implemented on Digital UNIX, and the Alpha architecture, 
offers the first and only 64-bit implementation providing the kind 
of performance customers need in business-critical computing 
environments," said Harbert. "We've tuned our offering, currently 
shipping as a developers' kit, to get the biggest benefit from the 
speed and price-performance of the Alpha microprocessor." 

     Digital intends to actively participate, as the CDE momentum 
continues into its next phase, as a sponsor of the CDE/Motif 
follow-on project. Digital, along with Fujitsu and Hitachi, is 
joining with the four original CDE developers in further evolving 
this important industry desktop technology and open standard.

     Two industry open systems organizations will be directly 
involved in the CDE/Motif project.  The Open Software Foundation 
(OSF) will coordinate the project, and the development and 
integration will be done by the X Consortium. Through the OSF/PST 
process, the seven CDE/Motif sponsors have agreed on a letter of 
intent describing the terms for the project. 

     Digital is also pleased to demonstrate its partnership with the 
other CDE vendors with its participation in the CDE activities at 
UniForum '95. Digital's CDE platforms are represented within the CDE 
pavilion (Booth #1646), the Internet Village and the Digital exhibit 
(Booth #1329.) Digital representatives are participating both in the 
pavilion as well as in CDE sessions throughout UniForum. 

