            DIGITAL STEALS SHOW IN 1995 "HOT IRON" AWARDS


         ... Products Take Gold in Six of 10 Categories ...


DALLAS, Texas -- March 15, 1995 -- For the second consecutive year, 
Digital Equipment Corporation has taken top honors in AIM 
Technology's 1995 Hot Iron Awards for Multiuser Shared UNIX Systems, 
announced here at UNIFORUM. Digital received six of of the 10 AIM 
performance and price/performance awards for its server, workstation 
and PC products.

     The Digital systems recognized by AIM include workstations and 
servers based on Digital's industry-leading 64-bit RISC-based Alpha 
microprocessors, and Digital's premier personal computer servers. 
               
Digital Hot Iron Awards for 1995 are: 
                  
	Best Throughput Performance, Multiuser Shared System Mix:
	Less than $50,000: DEC 3000 Model 700
	Over $150,000: DEC 7000 Model 760

	Best Price/Performance Throughput, Multiuser Shared System Mix:
	Less than $50,000: Prioris HX 590DP 
	Over $150,000: AlphaServer 2100 4/275

	Best Performer Workstation Mix:
	Less than $25,000: DEC 3000 Model 600
	Over $25,000: DEC 3000 Model 900

     In addition, Datamation magazine recently recognized the 
AlphaServer 2100 system with its prestigious Readers' Pick and 
Editors' Pick 1994 Server of the Year Award.

     "Once again, the AIM results demonstrate the sweeping 
performance and price/performance advantages that Digital's servers 
and workstations, both Alpha and Intel based, provide customers 
today," said Pauline Nist, Digital vice president, Alpha Servers 
Business. "The combination of Digital's UNIX, the world's most 
standards-compliant UNIX operating system, and Digital's Alpha and 
Prioris PC server systems provides users the performance, 
scalability and economy they expect for their most demanding 
business applications." 

     "Our second successive AIM Hot Iron Award provides further 
proof that the strength of Digital's Prioris PC server line is in 
the price/performance advantage customers gain in real-world network 
applications," said William Greenlund, Director of Digital's PC 
server product marketing.  "As we deliver on our commitment to 
provide higher levels of scalability and upgradeability in our 
server-designed products, customers will continue to receive the 
benefits of our unique value proposition." 

     Since 1981, AIM Technology has provided vendors and end users 
comprehensive, unbiased performance testing to help users determine 
the best fit between their application needs and available systems 
and configurations. 

     AIM's service helps users reduce the costs of evaluating new 
systems, and provides valuable broad-spectrum performance visibility 
for vendors that submit systems to be tested. AIM is an independent 
organization, as opposed to a vendor consortium, which allows AIM to 
bring an expert eye to performance measurement not restrained by 
objectives of consortium members.

