           BUSINESSES EAGER TO HAVE CABLE OPERATORS SUPPLY
               COMMUNICATIONS, DATA SERVICES OVER COAX


                    ...Digital Survey Reveals...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- March 14, 1994 -- A new survey has revealed that
businesses are extremely interested in having their high-speed data
communications needs supplied by cable television operators, Digital
Equipment Corporation announced today.  In addition, Digital has 
found that many cable operators are taking steps to prepare their 
plants for two-way, high-speed data communications to take advantage 
of this major new business opportunity. 

     "The speed, size, and enthusiasm of the response to the survey 
shows that business wants high-speed, multimedia communications over 
cable's broadband network, and wants it now," said Lois Levick, 
marketing manager of Emerging Network Technologies for Digital's 
Network Product Business.  "Digital is ideally positioned to become 
a market leader with its ChannelWorks products, which use the 
existing cable infrastructure to provide easy, economical networking 
and access to the Information Highway." 

Survey Reveals Many Potential Applications 

     Digital distributed 73,000 surveys to government, education, 
health care, and manufacturing companies, as well as to commercial 
Internet users, asking how they might apply high-speed data 
communications over cable and discussing the capabilities of 
ChannelWorks.  Whereas a typical survey response rate is 2%, to date 
13% of the surveys, or just over 9,500, have been returned. 

     The responses showed that corporations are interested in the 
cable network because its combination of high bandwidth and 
multipoint distribution allows them to implement multimedia 
applications such as manufacturing design, video teleconferencing, 
distance learning, and telemedicine, as well as to access the 
Internet and other on-line database services.  Many respondents also 
expressed interest in using cable for telecommuting, a capability 
that will be optimized with Digital's next product offering, which 
will be available later in 1995. 

     "People are eager to learn more about this new technology," 
said Levick.  "Many sent us personal notes saying they think 
business communications over cable is a great idea -- perhaps the 
most significant new development in information access.  They'd love 
to be able to use the existing cable infrastructure for the 
campus-wide and metropolitan-area networks and the multimedia 
applications they are currently implementing.  In fact, several 
respondents offered to speak to other community members and to their 
cable companies to help further the fast deployment of coax 
communications." 

Cable Operators See New Sources of Revenue

     In a second, complementary, campaign, Digital provided data on 
this new business opportunity to nearly 3,500 cable operators and 
offered an evaluation form to help them determine if their plants 
are ready for data communications. Technical experts at Digital and 
its channel partners will review the forms and work with cable 
companies to help them prepare for the new technology.  So far, the 
return rate has been 6.5%, with 230 requests for the form. 

     "Data communications over two-way activated cable is a 
tremendous business opportunity," said John Liskey, director of 
government and public affairs in Michigan for cable operator 
Tele-Communications, Inc., (TCI).  "Since last June, we've been 
successfully running several pilot projects in healthcare, 
government and education using ChannelWorks in East Lansing.  Now 
we're setting up a commercial business there and are actively 
signing up customers; the market is demand driven at this point.  
TCI's experience has made me a believer in the potential of the 
Information Highway." 

ChannelWorks Employs Existing Cable Infrastructure

     Digital's ChannelWorks family allows computer systems to make a 
standard 10MB Ethernet connection over existing cable television 
wiring at round-trip distances of up to 160 miles.  ChannelWorks, 
manufactured by LANcity, enables the creation of scalable, 
community-wide networks with a distributed rather than a point-to-
point configuration.  Any application that runs on a local area 
network in one building can now be run across an entire community to 
everyone else who is served by a ChannelWorks product. 

     Building on a strong tradition of technological excellence, 
Digital is leading the way in the networking industry today.  
Digital is the only company positioned by Datapro as a market leader 
for data over CATV, both in network access equipment and in network 
infrastructure.  And according to revenue figures from International 
Data Corporation (IDC), Dataquest, and Rising Star Research, Digital 
holds the number three market position in routers, hubs, and LAN 
switching markets, and holds the number two position in FDDI 
technology. Recently, the company pioneered a new, converged model 
for networking, in which hubs, routers, and switches are deployed 
on a single integrated platform.  In addition, Digital's expertise in 
network integration services has connected more than 12 million 
users worldwide, making Digital an ideal partner to the cable 
industry as it implements scalable networks for high-speed data 
communications. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software, and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
                                ####

