                    DIGITAL ANNOUNCES SUPPORT FOR
                    UNIVERSAL SERIAL BUS STANDARD


SAN FRANCISCO, Calif. -- March 20, 1995 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation today announced its support for the new Universal 
Serial Bus (USB) standard, which was introduced here at the Windows 
Hardware Engineering Conference (WinHEC) '95.

     The USB simplifies connectivity for new PC uses, such as 
computer integrated telephony.  In addition, PC users will benefit 
from the ease-of-use requirements for Plug and Play external devices 
and port consolidation, all at a very low cost.

     Digital joined the USB core group last summer and has been 
instrumental in the evolution of the specification since then.  The 
company is committed to the development of the USB specification.

     "As the PC begins connecting to higher bandwidth external 
resources, USB will be integral to Digital's strategy in delivering 
PC products that meet our customers' ease-of-use needs," said Scott 
Cutler, vice president, technology, for Digital's PC Business Unit.

     Bill Johnson, vice president, marketing and sales, Digital 
Semiconductor, added, "The Universal Serial Bus will enable PC users 
to add a wide variety of peripherals in a very straightforward and 
cost-effective manner.  Digital Semiconductor was actively involved 
in the creation of the USB standard, and it is our intention to 
incorporate USB support in our semiconductor products."

