        DIGITAL ANNOUNCES INDUSTRY'S FIRST PC-BASED SOLUTION
           TO COMBINE MULTIVENDOR NOS AND SNMP MANAGEMENT


MAYNARD, Mass. -- March 21, 1995 --  Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced ManageWORKS Workgroup Administrator, the industry's 
first management platform to combine multiple network operating 
system (NOS) administration with distributed simple network 
management protocol (SNMP) support in a single package.  With this 
introduction, supervisors responsible for administering a multi-
vendor environment now can easily and cost-effectively manage 
multiple PC networks and devices from a single console.  

     By reducing the hardware, training and personnel required to 
control multiple workgroups from different vendors, ManageWORKS 
Workgroup Administrator offers superior productivity with 
unsurpassed flexibility.  From a single Windows user interface, 
ManageWORKS provides local or remote management of Novell, IBM, 
Microsoft and Digital PC local area networks (LANs), as well as 
distributed SNMP hubs, bridges, routers, switches and PC servers, 
dramatically reducing wide area network management costs.   

     The unique, fully distributed nature of ManageWORKS allows 
administrators to share operational data anywhere on the network to 
improve management efficiency and planning. In addition, the ability 
to distribute SNMP management applications across PC LANs and global 
corporate networks reduces network traffic and cost.  This set of 
capabilities, previously available only in much more expensive 
solutions, provides ManageWORKS users with the tools to build an 
open, distributed, multi-level management environment.  

     "Our goal is to simplify the job of administrators who face the 
daunting task of managing multiple, heterogeneous local area 
networks," said Faye Allen, group marketing manager for Network 
Integration Software at Digital.  "By offering ManageWORKS Workgroup 
Administrator, we are extending our solution to reach all network 
managers who must not only cope with a mix of Novell, IBM, Microsoft 
and Digital PC networks, but also must manage the devices within 
those LANs."

     Along with the unveiling of ManageWORKS,  Digital announced 
that a network of 30 business partners have agreed to sell and 
distribute the product worldwide.  (See list attached.)   

     "This impressive list of partners is a reflection of Digital's 
commitment to expand our distribution efforts through indirect 
channels to reach a broader range of customers," said Allen.  "We 
expect that the number of partners will continue to grow as demand 
for the unique benefits and productivity of ManageWORKS Workgroup 
Administrator increases." 

     Digital offers a software developers' kit (SDK) and a 
comprehensive support program for independent software developers 
(ISVs) to integrate their applications with ManageWORKS, extending 
its functionality beyond multi-NOS and SNMP device management.  A 
number of applications for the ManageWORKS environment are 
available today, including NetCon asset management software from 
CapaCity, AuditWare network auditing software from Preferred 
Systems, HelpSTAR automated help desk reporting software from Help 
Desk Technology, the LANDesk Management Suite from Intel, and 
HUBwatch from Digital.  The ManageWORKS framework also is compliant 
with OLE 2.0, providing the foundation for further integration with 
other OLE 2.0-enabled applications.

     ManageWORKS is available today at a U.S. list price of $995.  
The SDK is priced at $195.  Upgrades for existing users of PATHWORKS 
ManageWORKS are priced at $495 until April 15 ($795 thereafter).  

     The minimum configuration for ManageWORKS Workgroup 
Administrator is a 386/33MHz PC with 8MB memory and Windows 3.1 or 
higher.  Installed easily on many laptop PCs, ManageWORKS also 
offers managers the ability to perform network troubleshooting in a 
mobile environment.  

