                 DIGITAL ANNOUNCES INDUSTRY-LEADING
                      BROADCAST VIDEO SOLUTION


         Allows major move from analog to digital technology


MAYNARD, Mass. -- March 21, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the introduction of an industry-leading, digital-
based, broadcast video solution called The AlphaStudio Broadcast 
System which will propel broadcast video technology into the 21st 
century.

     Using totally new digital video production capabilities based 
on 64-bit Alpha technology, this system is designed to meet the 
broadcast and related industrys' goals of phasing the video 
production environment from the standard analog technology so 
widely used today to full digital technology.

     The AlphaStudio Broadcast System is a completely digital 
broadcast video solution which provides major functional 
advancements and economies of operation compared to existing analog 
technology. Integrated high capacity and high performance storage 
with high performance computing provides powerful full motion 
digital imaging, and ensures reliability, productivity, and 
flexibility. 

The complete solution contains four major components:

     o  Automation Control - a communication system to control the 
        flow of video material.

     o 	Network Backbone - the software architecture which 
        efficiently distributes and manages the video data.

     o 	Broadcast Record/Edit/View (REV) System - which provides for 
        commercial playback, digital video disk recording, and non-
     	linear editing.

     o 	Library Server - which allows the video archive storing of 
        thousands of hours of video material.

In a more detailed look, the solution consists of: 

     o 	Digital's industry leading 64-bit AlphaServer and UNIX        
        software, and Digital's VELOCITOR, the industry's highest 
        speed storage disk subsystem.

     o 	A high quality broadcast digital video (CCIR 601) and audio 
        (AES) interface module from Leitch Technology Corporation of 
        Ontario, Canada.

     o 	A compatible automation control system from providers such 
        as Columbine JDS, Inc. of Golden, Colorado, and Louth 
        Automation of Menlo Park, California to manage commercial 
        and program scheduling, viewing, editing, and playback.

     o 	An automated tape library subsystem.

     The system from Digital is easily configured to fit each 
broadcaster's environment or specific requirements, and provides a 
state-of-the-art solution for video data capture, editing, 
archiving, and accessing needs in both broadcast and post-production 
environments.

Among its features are:

     o 	Allows record and play direct to and from disks with or 
        without the option of compression in multiple video streams.

     o 	Supports an open systems building block style of hardware 
        and software architecture with standard computer, storage, 
        network, video and software components.

     o 	Offers high availability by using hot swapping technology, 
        error correction code, and optional redundant system 
        components. 

     o 	Allows realtime management and manipulation of video data 
        for true random access in a non-linear operation.

     o 	Maintains original video quality after infinite repeated 
        broadcast or edits of stored information.

     o 	Reduces cost of ownership compared to analog technology, and 
        provides investment protection by incorporating existing 
        equipment.

     Gene Nelson, vice president of Digital's Computer Special 
Systems (CSS) group, noted that "in response to the explosive growth 
underway in the broadcast segment of the video market, Digital has 
made a major commitment to understanding and meeting the needs of 
broadcasters as they plan the transition from analog technology to 
digital technology.

     "We believe that our AlphaStudio Broadcast System provides a 
flexible, building-block strategy to help broadcast customers make 
that critical move toward a fully automated, computerized 
operation," he added.

     An initial pilot project for Digital's AlphaStudio Broadcast 
System is currently underway with WMUR-TV/Imes Communications, an 
ABC affiliate, in Manchester, New Hampshire.

     Emphasizing the significance of this new technology, Joe 
Paciorkowski, vice president of Engineering at WMUR-TV/Imes 
Communications, noted that "moving from analog to digital video
is similar to replacing a vinyl record album with a compact disc."

     Continuing, Paciorkowski said Digital was chosen as the "right 
company to handle our conversion process because of their ability to 
provide the highest quality digital video solution today, and their 
commitment to provide flexible broadcast solutions over the long 
term."

     Digital's AlphaStudio Broadcast System can be ordered 
immediately in the United States, and in late 1995 in Europe and 
Asia. 

     Each system will be configured to customer requirements, 
and prices range upward from $150,000. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
